                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "As a proof of concept, this game is fantastic: a third person action game with a bit of combat, exploration, simple puzzles, great music and atmosphere, all done in VR which gives it the depth of 3D that early flat screen 3D games could only aspire to. \n\nThe issue is with the execution: the character model of our hero looks waxy, and his animations are clunky and limited. The controls feel like molasses. There is only one enemy type you fight, and combat feels button mashy rather than deliberate. For example, you can light enemies on fire, but it’s never clear if timing is needed to do it, or you just need to keep swiping at them with your torch until they ignite. \n\nThe constantly switching fixed camera angles with each new room give it a feel exactly like an old PS2 game, like Rygar: you’re running down a hall, slip through a door, and the camera angle suddenly changes so that you have to push the joystick in a different direction to your initial run, even though you’re technically running in a straight line. Those of you who remember those old fixed camera games and don’t mind switching the angle of your joystick mid-run might get some nostalgia vibes from this game; otherwise, it can be jarring, janky, and a little annoying: you want to have control over the camera, but you just don’t.  \n\nThe game screams small team and small budget – and that’s not a criticism. What they’ve achieved with what was available is remarkable and unlike anything else I’ve played in VR (although others often mention Chronos in their reviews, which I’ve only played flat-screen). They had a vision and they executed on it as best they could. \n\nWhat they’ve achieved is to make me wish for more third person action games in VR made to be played with a controller. Imagine a God of War, Dead Space, Castlevania, or oh yes! even a Dark Souls with snappy animations, huge enemy variety, fast combat with various move sets and weapons, and labyrinthine level designs through multiple environments. Theseus paves the way for what could potentially be my new favourite genre in VR, given a larger scope.\n\nSo I give Theseus a cautious recommendation. The biggest grievance is its length, at around 2 hours to complete the story, though there are collectibles to get the “true” ending. But as a proof of concept and an atmospheric VR adventure, it’s well worth a playthrough, and enough to wet the appetite for this type of game that hopefully other developers will deliver on the promise of what Theseus has to offer. Grab it at a deep discount and it’s well worth your time. \n\nPlayed on Steam Frame with a PS5 Dual Sense controller.",
